statement:
Let $S$ and $A$ be affine schemes [def-pc-scheme], with $S$ Noetherian [pqf-def-locnoeth], and let $A\to S$ be flat, surjective and quasi-compact [def-weil-pbc-fpqc-covering]. Let $F$ be an fpqc sheaf of sets on $S$-schemes (a contravariant functor satisfying the covering equalizer condition), represented over $A$ by a scheme $P\to A$ locally of finite presentation [def-pc-representable-functor, def-sbc-locally-finite-presentation]. Let $T\to S$ be integral and surjective [def-weil-finite-integral-morphism], and suppose $F_T$ is represented by $Y$. On $R=Y\times_S T$ [def-pc-fibre-product], let $r_0,r_1:R\rightrightarrows Y$ be the projection and the map from the canonical identification of the two restrictions of $F$. Assume every $r_1(r_0^{-1}(y))$ is contained in a quasi-affine open subscheme of $Y$, that is, an open subscheme isomorphic to a quasi-compact open subscheme of an affine scheme (for instance an affine open). For each $x\in P$, there exist the following data:
(1) a subfunctor $F_0\subseteq F$ whose pullback to $A$ is represented by an open subscheme $P_0\subseteq P$ containing $x$, and whose pullback to $T$ is represented by an open subscheme $Y_0\subseteq Y$ [def-weil-open-immersion];
(2) an affine scheme $B$ of finite presentation over $S$, a map $a:A\to B$ over $S$, an affine scheme $Q$ finite and surjective over $S$, and a factorization $T\to Q\to S$;
(3) a separated finitely presented $B$-scheme $X$ and a separated finitely presented $Q$-scheme $Z$, together with identifications $P_0\simeq X\times_B A$, $F_{0,B}\simeq h_X$, and $F_{0,Q}\simeq h_Z$. In particular the identification $Z_B=Z\times_S B\simeq X\times_S Q$ gives a finite integral surjection $g:Z_B\to X$. Write $v:Z_B\to Z$ for the other projection. The canonical descent datum $D$ on $Z$ relative to $Q/S$ [def-pd-descent-datum] becomes under this identification the canonical datum on $X\times_S Q$ relative to $Q\times_S B/B$;
(4) an affine scheme $L$ with a quasi-compact flat monomorphism $j:L\to Z$ [def-weil-flat-morphism, def-weil-finite-type-morphism]. A monomorphism means that its diagonal is an isomorphism. Its image is stable under generalization (it contains every generalization of each of its points), and the two pullbacks of $j$ under $D$ agree as subfunctors. Let $j_B:L\times_S B\to Z_B$ be its base change. The point of $X$ which is the image of $x$ belongs to $g(j_B(L\times_S B))$.
Here finite presentation means locally of finite presentation, quasi-compact and quasi-separated, and separated means that the diagonal is a closed immersion [def-weil-quasi-separated-morphism, def-weil-closed-immersion]. No quasi-affine neighbourhood of $j(L)$ or representing scheme over $S$ is part of the conclusion.
